**/CaseEvaluationDbContextFactory.cs
**/CaseEvaluationTenantDbContextFactory.cs
**/CaseEvaluationDbContextFactoryBase.cs

# NOT OUR CODE, and NOT OUR COVERAGE. Added 2026-09-03 after reading the first
# real Cobertura artefact rather than assuming what was in it.
#
# `dotnet-coverage` writes ABSOLUTE runner paths, and the report carried two
# populations that are not this repository's source at all:
#
#   131 files of FluentValidation's OWN source, embedded by SourceLink and
#       rooted at `/_/` (e.g. /_/src/FluentValidation/AbstractValidator.cs)
#    70 build intermediates under obj/, chiefly Riok.Mapperly's generated
#       mappers (obj/Release/net10.0/Riok.Mapperly/**/*.g.cs)
#
# Measured on the artefact from CI run 33800844354 (PR #532, e87ee4c0):
#
#   scripts/coverage-gate.py --cobertura coverage.cobertura.xml --measure-only
#     with this list before these two lines : 66.24% (35049/52915) over 1082 files
#     after                                 : 73.61% (33195/45098) over  887 files
#
# The 7.37-point difference is a third-party library and a code generator being
# graded as ours. Worse than inaccurate, it DRIFTS: upgrade FluentValidation or
# change a Mapperly mapping and the measured figure moves with no change to any
# code we wrote. A floor that moves when a dependency moves is not a floor.
#
# 887 is the cross-check: it is exactly the number of tracked, non-excluded .cs
# files in this repo that the artefact contains, so these two patterns remove
# the contamination and nothing of ours.
# WHY `**/_/**` AND NOT `_/**`. The raw Cobertura path has a LEADING SLASH
# (`/_/src/FluentValidation/...`). `_/**` matches only after normalise() strips
# it, so it would work today and silently stop matching if --cobertura-prefix
# were ever set -- the contamination would return with no error and no symptom,
# which is the same "looks configured, is not" class this entry exists to fix.
# `**/_/**` matches the leading-slash form, the stripped form AND a prefixed
# form, so the exclusion no longer depends on a normalisation step it does not
# control. Verified against the artefact: it matches exactly the 131
# FluentValidation files and zero files of ours.
**/_/**

# ALSO NOT OUR CODE. Added 2026-09-04 (phase 3 item APP-OWN-01) after measuring,
# not after assuming -- and it was found only because the measurement was run
# TWICE, against a control.
#
# Referencing the AuthServer from Application.Tests (so the tenant-resolver chain
# can be asserted in both processes) loads assemblies that drag MessagePack's
# source-generator source into the report via SourceLink, rooted at
# `/home/runner/work/MessagePack-CSharp/...`. It is not caught by `**/_/**`
# above, because that matches FluentValidation's SourceLink root and this is a
# different one.
#
# Measured on two full `dotnet-coverage collect` runs, CI's pinned 18.11.0:
#
#   CONTROL, unmodified tree : 73.61% (33195/45098 over 887 files)
#     -- reproduces CI exactly, so the probe below is trustworthy
#   PROBE, AuthServer loaded : 68.25% (33831/49566 over 961 files)
#     of which NOT OURS      : 17.13% (  635/ 3706 over  45 files)
#     our code only          : 72.39% (33196/45860 over 916 files)
#
# So five of those points were never AuthServer. Without this line the backend
# floor would have been set from 68.25% and would then DRIFT on every MessagePack
# bump -- a floor that moves when a dependency moves, which is the same sentence
# the FluentValidation entry above already uses.
#
# WHY THE LEADING `**/`, same reasoning as `**/_/**`: the raw Cobertura path has a
# LEADING SLASH. `MessagePack-CSharp/**` would match only after normalise() strips
# it, so it would work today and silently stop matching if --cobertura-prefix were
# ever set. `**/MessagePack-CSharp/**` matches the leading-slash form, the stripped
# form AND a prefixed form, so it does not depend on a normalisation step it does
# not control.
#
# THIS IS THE THIRD NAMED VENDOR AND THAT IS THE PROBLEM, not the solution.
# SourceLink roots differ per vendor, so the next dependency will match none of
# these patterns and nothing will report it. Issue #683 tracks the class-level fix
# (assert every counted file is repo-tracked); Adrian decided to name this one now
# so the tenancy work lands with an honest floor, and to sequence the general fix
# separately rather than as a rider on this pull request.
**/MessagePack-CSharp/**

# Build output. `**/obj/**` is the one that mattered -- 64 Riok.Mapperly
# generated mappers were being graded as our source. `**/bin/**` matches nothing
# in today's report and is here because it is the SAME CLASS, and the reason obj/
# was missed is instructive: sonarcloud.yml already excluded both, but in
# `sonar.exclusions` (what to analyse) rather than `sonar.coverage.exclusions`
# (what to exclude from coverage). This list was seeded from the coverage half
# only, so it inherited half a configuration. Naming the whole class here is what
# stops the next generator that writes into bin/ from repeating it.
#
# Deliberately NOT copied across: node_modules/** and dist/**. They appear in
# sonar.exclusions but cannot appear in either coverage report -- karma
# instruments angular/src only -- and an entry that can never match is noise in
# a list whose every line is supposed to carry a reason.
**/obj/**
**/bin/**

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase 8.1 (2026-09-09): the DENOMINATOR decision. Adrian's ruling.
#
# Phase 8 targets 90% line coverage, so before measuring anything the question
# had to be settled: does the denominator describe application code? 1,842
# uncovered lines sat in `tools`, `scripts`, `.claude/scripts`,
# `docker/packet-renderer` and `tests/e2e-demo`.
#
# THE ANSWER WAS MOSTLY "IT BELONGS", and that is the part worth recording,
# because the cheap move was to exclude all of it and watch the percentage rise.
# 1,294 of the 1,842 stay IN and remain uncovered DELIBERATELY, pending Python
# test infrastructure that does not exist in this repository yet:
#
#   docker/packet-renderer/app.py          44  a PRODUCTION gunicorn service --
#                                              docker-compose.prod.yml:144, reached at
#                                              PacketRenderer__Url, renders the
#                                              PHI-bearing packet PDFs
#   tools/packet-templates                882  COPIED INTO that image and RUN at build
#                                              (Dockerfile:4, :60) -- its output ships
#   scripts/coverage-gate.py              223  this list's own second consumer, and the
#                                              instrument every phase 8 figure comes from
#   .claude/scripts/verify_structure.py   145  BLOCKING CI gate (ci.yml:309, no `|| true`)
#
# Only 548 lines are excluded, below. Each is named, none is a wildcard.
#
# WHICH CONSUMER THESE ACTUALLY AFFECT, stated because the header above insists
# every line carries a reason and warns that an entry which can never match is
# noise. These four are NOT that case, but they are not the usual case either:
#
#   SonarCloud job      APPLIES them. Sonar analyses Python and TypeScript, counts
#                       these files, and reports them 100% uncovered today.
#   coverage-gate.py    READS them and they match nothing, because it ingests only
#                       --lcov (karma, angular/src) and --cobertura (.NET). No Python
#                       enters either report, and tests/e2e-demo is a separate
#                       Playwright project outside the Angular lcov.
#
# That is different from node_modules/dist, which were deliberately left out
# because they can match in NEITHER consumer. These match in one, which is the
# whole reason they are here: without them Sonar's denominator counts tooling
# that no runner will ever cover.

# Manual regeneration only. docs/repo-map/README.md:47 -- "Regeneration is manual
# via the build-repo-map.py script". Nothing invokes it at build or in CI, so a
# test of it would protect nothing that can break the build.
.claude/scripts/build-repo-map.py

