.claude/scripts/check-links.py

# Test helpers, not application code. Playwright fixtures for the demo E2E project
# (its own package.json, outside the Angular workspace). Coverage of the instrument
# rather than the subject; measuring it would be circular.
tests/e2e-demo/**

# Test code, not application code -- the same reasoning as tests/e2e-demo above,
# for the Python suite added by #784. Sonar's Python analyzer does NOT classify
# test files as tests: it reports these five as FIL (ordinary source), unlike the
# C# and TypeScript analyzers which give their test files UTS. So without this
# entry their 251 lines enter `lines_to_cover` and read 0% forever, and every
# further test written to raise a module's coverage would push its own target
# further away.
#
# MEASURED, because this list forbids an entry that can never match: the gate's
# own glob_to_regex maps `tests/python/**` onto all five files and onto none of
# scripts/coverage-gate.py, .claude/scripts/verify_structure.py or
# tools/packet-templates/**. And exclusion genuinely removes a file from the
# denominator -- `**/Program.cs` is in this list and both Program.cs files report
# ncloc with NO lines_to_cover at all.
#
# This is `sonar.coverage.exclusions`, NOT `sonar.exclusions`: Sonar still
# analyses these files for issues. Only the coverage denominator changes.
tests/python/**

# Test code, not application code -- the same reasoning as tests/python/** above,
# for the golden-output suite #821 added. Sonar's Python analyzer reports these as
# FIL (ordinary source) rather than UTS, so without this entry test_golden_output.py
# is graded as uncovered application code and any pull request touching it fails
# `new_coverage 0.0 < 80`. #821 merged with its own gate red for exactly this, and
# it recurs on every future Python pull request until this line exists.
#
# LIVE TODAY, read from SonarCloud rather than predicted:
#
#   tools/packet-templates/tests   lines_to_cover 70   uncovered_lines 70   ncloc 110
#
# MEASURED, because this list forbids an entry that can never match -- and here the
# sharper risk is one that matches TOO MUCH. Run through the gate's own
# glob_to_regex (scripts/coverage-gate.py:42) against all 3,398 tracked files:
#
#   matches EXACTLY 2   tools/packet-templates/tests/test_golden_output.py
#                       tools/packet-templates/tests/golden.sha256
#   matches 0 of the 14 production files under tools/packet-templates/
#   matches none of scripts/coverage-gate.py, .claude/scripts/verify_structure.py
#                or docker/packet-renderer/app.py
#
# The second line is the load-bearing one. Phase 8.1 above ruled that the 882 lines
# in tools/packet-templates stay IN the denominator deliberately, because they ship
# inside the production image. This entry must not reach them, and does not.
# golden.sha256 is a hash fixture no analyzer can cover; it is named only because
# the glob reaches it.
#
# PROVEN, not proposed: #835 ran this same experiment for tests/python/** and took
# `SonarCloud Code Analysis` from FAILURE to SUCCESS, with the file still analysed
# for issues (qualifier=FIL, ncloc=84) and simply carrying no coverable lines. This
# is sonar.coverage.exclusions, NOT sonar.exclusions -- analysis is unaffected.
tools/packet-templates/tests/**

# Test code, not application code -- the C# suites, and the entry the three above
# should have had from the start. They are `tests/` PLURAL; the C# projects live in
# `test/` SINGULAR and matched none of them, so every C# test file has been sitting
# in the backend denominator at 99.22%, because test code is covered by construction:
# running the suite executes it.
#
# MEASURED at c035a2a0, one report and one run, before and after this entry:
#
#   ALL (what the gate reported)   44331/53631 = 82.66%   over 971 files
#     test code only               26730/26940 = 99.22%   over 326 files
#     PRODUCTION only              17601/26691 = 65.94%   over 645 files
#
# Both halves reconcile to the gate's own totals. FLOOR_BACKEND moved 72 -> 65 in the
# same change; it has to, or CI fails on the first run after merge.
#
# `test/**` WOULD MATCH NOTHING, AND THAT IS THE TRAP THIS ENTRY EXISTS TO AVOID.
# glob_to_regex (scripts/coverage-gate.py:54-80) returns an ANCHORED regex and
# excluded() at :272 uses .match, while the .NET Cobertura carries ABSOLUTE checkout
# paths (see the in_repo docstring at :181-204). So:
#
#   test/**       -> ^test(?:/.*)?$           vs C:/.../test/X.Tests/Y.cs   NO MATCH
#   **/test/**    -> ^(?:.*/)?test(?:/.*)?$   vs the same path              MATCH
#
# The three entries above are written root-relative because the Python and Angular
# reports ARE repo-relative. The .NET one is not, and the obvious transcription of
# their style would have merged and changed the figure by zero.
#
# MEASURED against the gate's own imported matcher, not an approximation of it:
#   **/test/**      excludes all 326 C# test files
#                   Application.Tests 150, Domain.Tests 89, EFCore.Tests 63, TestBase 24
#                   production files also caught: NONE, checked explicitly
#   **/*.Tests/**   was rejected: it excludes 302 and leaves TestBase's 24 counted, and
#                   a naming convention silently re-admits any future project not
#                   named *.Tests -- the same class of miss that caused this defect.
#
# WHY THE FILE COUNT IN ci.yml's FLOOR_BACKEND COMMENT HAD ALREADY DRIFTED. That block
# named 916 as its load-bearing count and 961 as the contamination signal; the gate
# reported 971, so the safeguard had gone PAST its own trigger and could never fire
# again while still reading as armed. Accounted for rather than assumed:
#
#   916 + 56 report entries added since a8e221df - 1 deleted = 971   (exact)
#   of those 56: 52 under test/, 4 under src/
#   87 .cs files were added in that span; 31 never enter the report at all
#     (migrations, Designer files, and const-only files with no instrumentable code)
#
# So 93% of the drift was test files, which means the tripwire could not have held
# while they were counted -- this epic's tranches add test files continuously. After
# this entry, 645 moves only when PRODUCTION files change, which is what a
# contamination detector should key on. That is the argument for this change, not a
# side effect of it.
#
# This is `sonar.coverage.exclusions`, NOT `sonar.exclusions`: the `sonarcloud` job in ci.yml
# greps this file into COVERAGE_EXCLUSIONS and feeds it to sonar.coverage.exclusions only. Sonar keeps
# analysing these files for issues; only the coverage denominator changes.
**/test/**

